   "text": "I read this as a cardenolide: a digitoxigenin-type steroid with a butenolide at C17, a 14-hydroxyl, and a chain of three 2,6-dideoxy sugars on the 3-oxygen. The 5-H, 8-H, 9-H, both angular methyls, the 14-OH, the C17 lactone bond and the 3-O bond are all drawn with wedges or hashes, so I assigned chirality at every one of those centres. For each stereocentre I took the wedge/hash geometry from the drawing and converted it to @/@@ by a signed-volume test. This gives the stereochemistry as drawn, which differs from the usual digitoxin configuration at several centres. I did not check it against a reference structure.\n\n**Steroid core.** Ring A is a cyclohexane, ring B a cyclohexane with the 5-H, and ring C a cyclohexane carrying the 14-OH. Ring D is a cyclopentane. There are methyls on C10 and C13. The lactone is C1=CC(=O)OC1 joined at C17.\n\n**Sugar chain.** Each sugar is a tetrahydropyran with a 6-methyl and a ring CH2. The terminal ring carries 3-OH and 4-OH. The two inner rings each carry a 3-OH and a glycosidic oxygen on C4. They are linked 1\u21924 to each other and to the aglycone's 3-oxygen.\n\nIn the SMILES, the sugar rings use digits 1\u20133. The steroid uses digits 4\u20138 so that no ring-closure digit is reused while it is still open.\n\nSMILES: C[C@@H]1[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)C[C@H](O[C@@H]2[C@@H](C)O[C@@H](O[C@@H]3[C@@H](C)O[C@@H](O[C@@H]4C[C@H]5CC[C@@H]6[C@@H]([C@@]5(C)CC4)CC[C@@]7(C)[C@]6(O)CC[C@@H]7C8=CC(=O)OC8)C[C@@H]3O)C[C@@H]2O)O1"
